Q: Java Thread: Real world Application Example A: <h2>Modern Java</h2>
<p>Here is a solution based on the correct <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/32332247/642706">Answer by Houssam Badri</a> but using newer features added to Java in later years.</p>
<h3><code>List.of</code></h3>
<p>Define your list of input integers. The <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/List.html#of(E,E,E,E)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>List.of</code></a> method is a convention way to instantiate an <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/List.html#unmodifiable" rel="nofollow noreferrer">unmodifiable list</a> in a literals fashion.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/SequencedCollection.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SequencedCollection</code></a> is now the super-interface of <code>List</code>.</p>
<pre><code>SequencedCollection &lt; Integer &gt; numbers = List.of( 1 , 2 , 3 ) ;
</code></pre>
<h3><code>ExecutorService</code></h3>
<p>We can use a <code>ExecutorService</code> to manage our threads. No need to juggle <code>Thread</code> manually.</p>
<p>The easy way to instantiate an executor service is through the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/Executors.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Executors</code></a> utility class.</p>
<h4>try-with-resources</h4>
<p>We can use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/tryResourceClose.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">try-with-resources</a> syntax to automatically close our executor service after our submitted tasks complete.</p>
<h4><code>Runnable</code> lambda</h4>
<p>You submit your tasks to the executor service as objects implementing either <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/lang/Runnable.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Runnable</code></a> or <code>Callable</code>. The second of those returns a result, the first does not. As a shortcut, you can submit a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/java/javaOO/lambdaexpressions.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">lambda</a>, as seen below.</p>
<h3><code>List#forEach</code></h3>
<p>Calling <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/lang/Iterable.html#forEach(java.util.function.Consumer)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>List#forEach</code></a> is a easy way to loop elements and execute some code for each.</p>
<h3>Virtual threads</h3>
<p>If you have a great number of tasks, and their code involves significant amounts of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blocking_(computing)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">blocking</a> (that is, they are not <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPU-bound" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CPU-bound</a>), then use <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" rel="nofollow noreferrer">virtual threads</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>SequencedCollection &lt; Integer &gt; numbers = List.of( 1 , 2 , 3 );
try (
        ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor() ;
)
{
    numbers.forEach( integer -&gt; executorService.submit( ( ) -&gt; IO.println( integer ) ) );
}
// At this point all submitted tasks are done.
// The executor service is automatically shut down.
</code></pre>
<h3>Caveat</h3>
<p>As explained in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79892099/642706">Answer by Stephen C</a>, the particulars of this example scenario do not justify multi-threading. But it does serve as a simple demonstration of multi-threading for newbies.</p>
